00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:22.833
Let's dive into this big topic of pirates.

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:26.070
Pirates aren't one thing.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:27.570
They've always existed.

00:05:27.570 --> 00:05:30.000
As long as there's been
international trade on the ocean,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:31.890
there have been pirates,

00:05:31.890 --> 00:05:35.220
sailors who utilize the
expanse of the open ocean

00:05:35.220 --> 00:05:39.813
to rob and steal and run
sophisticated businesses.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:43.950
If you haven't seen CGP
Grey's deep dive on pirates,

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:44.783
go watch it.

00:05:44.783 --> 00:05:46.410
It's a two-parter.

00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:49.050
Pirates are everything from Norse sailors

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.480
who raided other ships on the high seas

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:53.550
to the famous pirates of the Caribbean,

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:55.410
like Britain's Blackbeard,

00:05:55.410 --> 00:05:57.120
who stole a French slave ship

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.033
and souped it up into the
ultimate pirate vessel.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.120
Pirates were poor men

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:05.970
who were looking for
money and social mobility.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:09.360
Some of them were looking
for fame and glory and power

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.130
in a time where seafaring men

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:14.730
had little status or
opportunity in society.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:16.200
We've romanticized them,

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.490
we've caricatured them,

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:21.510
and we idealize them as
legends and myths of the past.

00:06:21.510 --> 00:06:24.420
But piracy never fully went away.

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:26.973
It still very much exists today.

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.420
Here's a map of all the piracy incidents

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.400
over the past 40 years,

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:34.140
at least the reported ones.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:36.630
From Indonesian pirates
hijacking cargo ships

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:38.190
in the Strait of Malacca

00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:40.800
to Bangladeshi pirates
holding fishermen hostage

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.540
in exchange for ransoms,

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:43.920
all the way to West Africa,

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.580
where Nigerian pirates
steal oil off huge tankers

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.180
and sell it on the black market.

00:06:50.370 --> 00:06:53.160
But most of the dots on
this map are right here,

00:06:53.160 --> 00:06:55.170
the gateway to the Suez Canal,

00:06:55.170 --> 00:06:57.750
one of the busiest
highways of global trade,

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.190
off the coast of a country

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.803
where piracy uniquely could thrive.

00:07:04.350 --> 00:07:07.533
The government of Somalia
collapsed in the early '90's,

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:09.690
the country fractured,

00:07:09.690 --> 00:07:11.520
and instead of a central government,

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.880
local clans started to control
patches of the country.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.080
There was no real government

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:19.020
to regulate this
3,000-kilometer coastline,

00:07:19.020 --> 00:07:20.763
the longest coastline in Africa.

00:07:21.990 --> 00:07:24.240
As a result, huge fishing boats,

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:26.010
mostly from Iran and Yemen,

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.960
took advantage of this free-for-all,

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:31.743
swooping in to these unregulated
Somali waters to fish.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:34.770
These foreign fishers would steal

00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:37.770
hundreds of millions of
dollars of seafood every year

00:07:37.770 --> 00:07:39.990
with nets that tear up the sea floor,

00:07:39.990 --> 00:07:41.490
destroying the ecosystem

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.670
and depleting the fish
stock in the process.

00:07:44.670 --> 00:07:47.190
This illegal fishing by foreign vessels

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.860
started to outsize the catch of locals

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.510
whose water this was.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.210
Local Somali fishermen
with their tiny boats

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.350
couldn't compete,

00:07:55.350 --> 00:07:57.750
and soon this illegal foreign fishing

00:07:57.750 --> 00:08:00.213
eclipsed the catch of local Somalis.

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:02.610
Around the same time,

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:03.960
companies from Switzerland

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.330
and Somali's former colonizer Italy

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:08.670
were illegally paying corrupt factions

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:09.810
of the Somali government

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:11.760
to take their toxic waste,

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:13.953
some of it even being radioactive.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.620
Tons of this waste ended up

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:19.140
in both Somalia's land and water,

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:21.090
which would go on to
have significant effects

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.133
on the health of local communities.

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.460
So it's the 1990s,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:32.670
and between the illegal
overfishing and the waste dumping,

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:34.530
Somali people's main livelihood

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.540
is being pillaged by foreigners.

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.240
And they don't really have a
government to help them out,

00:08:39.240 --> 00:08:40.080
to protect them.

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:42.900
So some Somalis decided to fight back.

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:46.020
- An American cargo ship
taken over by pirates.

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:47.280
- Somali fishermen got together,

00:08:47.280 --> 00:08:49.170
and they formed an ocean militia.

00:08:49.170 --> 00:08:51.390
Some would call themselves
the Coast Guard,

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:53.250
and they would use these tiny boats

00:08:53.250 --> 00:08:55.620
to chase down foreign fishing vessels

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.600
and demand that they pay a fine.

00:08:57.600 --> 00:09:00.510
Some experts have called
this subsistence piracy

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.860
because it was piracy,

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:04.950
but it was also kind of fishermen
defending their livelihood

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:06.420
from predatory foreigners.

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:08.250
And at first it was rare.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:09.480
I mean, if you look at the data,

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:13.200
you just see a handful of
successful incidents every year.

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:15.123
Oh, and they were pretty basic too.

00:09:18.060 --> 00:09:20.430
Like in one of these early
incidents of Somali piracy

00:09:20.430 --> 00:09:22.260
back in like 1994,

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:25.590
you've got this wooden
boat full of 26 Somali men

00:09:25.590 --> 00:09:28.410
claiming to be the Somali Coast Guard,

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:30.480
and they go up to a shipping vessel

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.680
and they get on board.

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:33.720
They like hijack it for five days,

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:35.250
but realize they can't really do anything

00:09:35.250 --> 00:09:38.400
so they just steal as much as
they can from the cargo ship

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:39.903
and just go on their way.

00:09:41.670 --> 00:09:43.200
Yes, this was piracy,

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.930
but it was super basic
and kind of harmless.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:49.050
It was a bunch of locals
filling in on the power vacuum

00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:50.850
left by their non-existent government.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:53.370
And this is how it was for a long time.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.190
Loosely organized gangs
of armed Somali men

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.770
climbing aboard cargo
ships or fishing vessels,

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.140
intimidating the crews to collect fines

00:10:01.140 --> 00:10:03.930
or sometimes asking for
informal license fees

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:05.220
for fishing in their waters

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:06.360
even though they didn't have any authority

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:07.680
to give out licenses.

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:08.880
They did it to get money,

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:09.713
to basically be like,

00:10:09.713 --> 00:10:10.627
"If you're gonna fish in our waters,

00:10:10.627 --> 00:10:11.580
"you need to pay us."

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:13.710
And the rest of the world
didn't really know about this.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.990
It like wasn't big enough
news for anyone to care.

00:10:15.990 --> 00:10:17.703
But that would soon change.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.240
- Across the region,
80,000 people are dead.

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:28.230
At least 22,000 of them were
killed here in Sri Lanka.

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:29.790
- So there's kind of a turning point.

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:31.590
Christmas 2004.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:33.510
This tsunami was devastating,

00:10:33.510 --> 00:10:35.310
mostly in Southeast Asia,

00:10:35.310 --> 00:10:38.880
but it also hit Somalia's
really delicate coast,

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:40.500
destroying homes and boats

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:44.280
and crippling an already
delicate economy and food supply.

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:45.750
- [Reporter] The tsunami took the lives

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:48.210
of some 150 people in Somalia

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.060
and poisoned the precious
little water supply

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.380
that people had here.

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.440
- And it also washed up
some of that toxic waste

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:57.480
that had been dumped off their shores.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.640
This led to a UN investigation,

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:01.380
which concluded that the waste

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:03.600
was likely causing severe damage

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:05.370
to the health of local people.

00:11:05.370 --> 00:11:06.300
And at this point,

00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:08.820
Somalia still isn't one country.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:09.900
There's some parts of the country

00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:11.100
that are peaceful and safe

00:11:11.100 --> 00:11:12.240
and under government control,

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.490
but most of Somalia is still a patchwork

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:16.830
of competing clans and warlords,

00:11:16.830 --> 00:11:19.830
a hotbed for terrorism and
organized crime networks

00:11:19.830 --> 00:11:22.023
that controlled different
parts of the country.

00:11:22.023 --> 00:11:25.530
(soldier chanting)

00:11:25.530 --> 00:11:27.810
And piracy, this thing that started off

00:11:27.810 --> 00:11:30.510
as an informal Coast Guard,

00:11:30.510 --> 00:11:33.964
was maturing in sophisticated ways.

00:11:33.964 --> 00:11:37.631
(ethereal electronic music)

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:43.020
By 2005, you start to see
pirates using motherships,

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:44.490
larger ships that allow them

00:11:44.490 --> 00:11:46.710
to go way further off their coast,

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:48.840
and then to use speedboats to attack

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:50.580
like they did to this cargo ship.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:53.553
This was a huge level-up
for Somali pirates.

00:11:55.500 --> 00:11:58.140
These motherships started
to be equipped with radar,

00:11:58.140 --> 00:11:59.160
which allowed the pirates

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.830
to have new ways of detecting their prey.

00:12:01.830 --> 00:12:02.730
You start to see them have

00:12:02.730 --> 00:12:06.090
GPS systems or satellite
phones to communicate.

00:12:06.090 --> 00:12:07.110
Pirates start looking at

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:09.600
shipping industry blogs and databases

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:11.850
to locate and track shipping vessels.

00:12:11.850 --> 00:12:14.910
This was turning from
basic subsistence piracy

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:16.590
to something more sophisticated.

00:12:16.590 --> 00:12:20.280
Pirates started to realize
just how much money and value

00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:22.680
was flowing through this corridor

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:24.510
headed towards the Suez Canal,

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:27.660
like one of the busiest
highways of international trade

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:28.560
in the world.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:30.510
And with these new and improved tactics

00:12:30.510 --> 00:12:32.790
came more profitable business models

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:35.070
that would change everything for pirates.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:36.260
- [Reporter] Police in
Finland say the owner

00:12:36.260 --> 00:12:38.460
of the missing freighter Arctic Sea

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:40.860
has received a demand for a ransom.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:42.450
- It was the early 2000's,

00:12:42.450 --> 00:12:45.840
and it was the beginning
of the era of ransoms.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:49.290
- You were kidnapped by Somali pirates

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.590
and you were held hostage
for more than two years.

00:12:52.590 --> 00:12:54.210
- [Johnny] Hey, Michael,
it's Johnny Harris.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:55.980
- [Michael] Hi, Johnny,
good to hear from you.

00:12:55.980 --> 00:12:57.510
- So we had a really
interesting conversation

00:12:57.510 --> 00:12:59.040
with this American journalist

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:00.810
named Michael Scott Moore.

00:13:00.810 --> 00:13:02.910
He was kidnapped by Somali pirates

00:13:02.910 --> 00:13:05.160
and held for almost three years

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.850
before being released for
a $1.6 million ransom.

00:13:08.850 --> 00:13:09.683
- [Michael] And people say,

00:13:09.683 --> 00:13:12.270
"Oh, but they're just
frustrated fishermen."

00:13:12.270 --> 00:13:13.470
They're not.

00:13:13.470 --> 00:13:14.610
They were in the '90's,

00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:15.870
but it was a much bigger deal

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:18.240
than just fishermen turning to piracy.

00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:20.223
- Ransoms changed everything.

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:24.690
Pirates were realizing how easy it was

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.670
to hold a ship hostage

00:13:26.670 --> 00:13:29.160
and to demand a multimillion dollar ransom

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.320
to extract a huge profit,

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:34.110
which attracted the attention of warlords,

00:13:34.110 --> 00:13:36.090
who saw a business opportunity.

00:13:36.090 --> 00:13:39.360
Warlords now wanted to fund hijackings

00:13:39.360 --> 00:13:40.230
like this one

00:13:40.230 --> 00:13:43.680
where pirates were hijacking
a Ukrainian coal ship

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:46.030
that was traveling from
South Africa to Turkey.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.670
They could hold it hostage and release it

00:13:50.670 --> 00:13:53.580
only in exchange for $700,000,

00:13:53.580 --> 00:13:54.630
which is what they got.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.630
Or another instance where
pirates riding on speedboats

00:13:57.630 --> 00:13:59.250
captured this Japanese ship

00:13:59.250 --> 00:14:01.020
and its 20-person crew,

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:02.160
holding them hostage

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.980
until somebody coughed up $2 million.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:07.950
There was investment in
these pirate operations

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:10.020
because there was a return to be had.

00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:13.620
And by 2007, around 30% of
the world's pirate attacks

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:15.333
occurred within this circle.

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:18.480
But wait, let's keep this in perspective.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.310
This actually wasn't particularly high.

00:14:20.310 --> 00:14:21.630
If you look at a graph

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:24.120
of all of the other regions of the world,

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:26.640
you'll see that this wasn't
by any means an outlier.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:28.980
But watch what happens the next year.

00:14:28.980 --> 00:14:30.330
This line going up

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:34.533
represents the expansion of
the piracy industry in Somalia.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:38.490
There was way too much
money to be made here,

00:14:38.490 --> 00:14:40.710
and warlords were
getting better and better

00:14:40.710 --> 00:14:43.890
at investing in and
running pirate businesses.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:47.160
By 2009, this body of water off of Somalia

00:14:47.160 --> 00:14:51.060
was home to nearly half of
all piracy incidents on Earth.

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:55.050
And look at this graph of
the average ransoms paid.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:58.980
In 2008, the average ransom
was a million dollars.

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:02.070
A total of $30 million
was paid out that year.

00:15:02.070 --> 00:15:03.360
And you gotta keep this in perspective,

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.760
these were huge sums of money for anyone,

00:15:05.760 --> 00:15:08.670
but especially poor
fishermen turned pirates

00:15:08.670 --> 00:15:13.440
in a country with a GDP per
person of $517 per year.

00:15:13.440 --> 00:15:14.850
But even still,

00:15:14.850 --> 00:15:16.110
this was just the beginning.

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:18.780
- If you'd simply refuse to pay,

00:15:18.780 --> 00:15:21.510
I mean, the pirates
have a lot of hostages.

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:24.900
The consequences of those
being shot, for instance,

00:15:24.900 --> 00:15:26.283
would be unimaginable.

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:30.210
- All of this influx of ransom cash

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:33.090
kicked off a pirate economy cycle

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:34.950
where pirates would attack a ship,

00:15:34.950 --> 00:15:36.120
hold the crew hostage,

00:15:36.120 --> 00:15:37.830
and get paid a high ransom.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:40.080
They would bring this back
to the clan leader warlord

00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:41.160
who funded them,

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:42.750
which would make that person rich

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.090
and allow them to invest in bigger boats

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:48.390
and more guns and
recruiting more young men

00:15:48.390 --> 00:15:50.610
and appeasing the local
coastal communities

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:51.900
who would support them,

00:15:51.900 --> 00:15:54.750
which they would use to hijack more ships

00:15:54.750 --> 00:15:56.490
in more sophisticated ways.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:58.530
And it repeated and repeated,

00:15:58.530 --> 00:16:01.830
getting more and more sophisticated
and dangerous each time.

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.500
- [Michael] So a businessman
who gets to be so rich

00:16:04.500 --> 00:16:08.493
that he needs to protect his
business interests with gunmen,

00:16:09.570 --> 00:16:11.217
that's a warlord.

00:16:11.217 --> 00:16:12.960
And a pirate boss is nothing else.

00:16:12.960 --> 00:16:15.630
He's a businessman who invests in piracy.

00:16:15.630 --> 00:16:18.000
- The pirate businesses
had lawyers and cooks

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:19.350
and bank-note tellers

00:16:19.350 --> 00:16:22.200
with machines that could detect fake bills

00:16:22.200 --> 00:16:24.000
so that they could know
if their ransom cash

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:24.900
was real or not.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:26.070
There was this great reporting

00:16:26.070 --> 00:16:29.190
at the time in 2009 by Reuters.

00:16:29.190 --> 00:16:31.500
One of their journalists
went to a pirate town

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:33.690
that had been a sleepy fishing village

00:16:33.690 --> 00:16:37.380
but had turned into a booming pirate hub,

00:16:37.380 --> 00:16:39.540
or in their words, pirate lair.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:43.320
What Reuters found was that
this economy was so booming

00:16:43.320 --> 00:16:45.300
that they had created a stock exchange

00:16:45.300 --> 00:16:48.720
where any local in the town
could invest what they had

00:16:48.720 --> 00:16:50.790
into a piracy business.

00:16:50.790 --> 00:16:52.800
There were dozens of
these piracy businesses,

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.060
and you can invest in the one

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:57.090
that you think is gonna
go get a successful ransom

00:16:57.090 --> 00:17:00.510
by hijacking an international
shipping vessel.

00:17:00.510 --> 00:17:01.950
And people made a lot of money.

00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:06.390
One local said they made
$75,000 in just a couple months

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:09.420
because they had made a good
bet on the right pirates.

00:17:09.420 --> 00:17:12.360
- [Michael] Pirate bosses
were not just top pirates,

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:16.083
they were investors who had
a portfolio of businesses.

00:17:17.220 --> 00:17:18.540
- These pirate businesses

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:21.330
would in turn support these communities,

00:17:21.330 --> 00:17:23.880
help fund the schools and the hospitals

00:17:23.880 --> 00:17:26.070
in order to curry favor with the locals

00:17:26.070 --> 00:17:27.540
to make sure that they would support them

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:28.590
running their businesses.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:30.720
But the people who were
really getting rich here

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:32.487
were mostly the clan leaders, the warlords

00:17:32.487 --> 00:17:36.210
who were building massive
villas and buying luxury cars

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:39.123
and throwing lavish, drug-laden parties.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.970
This was no longer the DIY Coast Guard.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:47.610
This was now lucrative organized crime.

00:17:47.610 --> 00:17:50.160
And yet some of these pirate groups

00:17:50.160 --> 00:17:52.410
still tried to make a moral case

00:17:52.410 --> 00:17:55.170
as to why they were
justified in doing this,

00:17:55.170 --> 00:17:56.910
saying that this wasn't piracy,

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:58.530
this was retaliation

00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:01.500
for all of the exploitation
of Somali waters

00:18:01.500 --> 00:18:03.003
by foreign countries.

00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:09.660
You see this in a 2008
hijacking of a Ukrainian vessel,

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.360
when these pirates demand $8 million,

00:18:12.360 --> 00:18:14.160
claiming that they would use the money

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:15.690
to clean up the toxic waste

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:18.330
that European companies
had dumped in their waters.

00:18:18.330 --> 00:18:22.470
Yes, European companies have
dumped toxic waste in Somalia.

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:25.110
Were these pirates actually
going to use the $8 million

00:18:25.110 --> 00:18:25.950
to clean that up?

00:18:25.950 --> 00:18:26.820
Probably not.

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:28.440
At least that's what we
found in our reporting.

00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:31.080
A lot of the moralistic,
like Robin Hood narrative

00:18:31.080 --> 00:18:33.840
became really thin once
warlords got involved

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:36.480
and once they demanded a
return for their investment.

00:18:36.480 --> 00:18:39.540
And you just start to see how
thin this moral argument is

00:18:39.540 --> 00:18:41.310
when pirates start boarding ships

00:18:41.310 --> 00:18:43.440
and just shooting crew at random

00:18:43.440 --> 00:18:44.940
to show how serious they were

00:18:44.940 --> 00:18:46.590
and to increase the likelihood

00:18:46.590 --> 00:18:48.360
of getting a high ransom payout.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:53.360
- Unfortunately, there is
one seaman from mainland

00:18:54.061 --> 00:18:56.790
who was killed by by the Somali pirates.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.160
- [Johnny] Or when they
started hijacking UN ships

00:18:59.160 --> 00:19:01.620
that were full of food and medical aid

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:03.450
meant for Somali people.

00:19:03.450 --> 00:19:06.000
- The ship apparently was
bound for Mombasa, Kenya,

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.970
and the shipment included
vegetable oil, corn, soy blend,

00:19:08.970 --> 00:19:10.560
and other basic food commodities

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:12.900
bound for people in countries

00:19:12.900 --> 00:19:14.550
including Somalia, Uganda, and Kenya.

00:19:14.550 --> 00:19:17.040
- This was about money and power,

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:18.420
and it was able to thrive

00:19:18.420 --> 00:19:21.270
because of a country that
didn't have a government

00:19:21.270 --> 00:19:22.830
that actually controlled the country.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:26.730
By 2011, there were 237
piracy incidents here.

00:19:26.730 --> 00:19:30.120
Over 1,200 hostages were taken that year.

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.703
35 of them died while in captivity.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:35.730
This was becoming more and more deadly

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:37.740
and more and more lucrative.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:39.060
Let's go back to that graph

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:41.760
of the average ransoms that were paid.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:45.300
You can see it grow up
to around $5 million

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:46.860
for the average ransom.

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:50.017
The total number of ransoms paid in 2011?

00:19:50.017 --> 00:19:52.023
$150 million.

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:57.270
It was getting out of hand,

00:19:57.270 --> 00:19:59.130
but it would take a few key events

00:19:59.130 --> 00:20:01.683
for the world to really take notice.

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:04.289
- [Captain] Ladies and gentlemen,

00:20:04.289 --> 00:20:05.122
ladies and gentlemen,

00:20:05.122 --> 00:20:07.860
all ships stay inside,
stay inside, stay inside.

00:20:07.860 --> 00:20:10.830
We are trying to run away from them.

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:12.968
Now, stay inside everybody.

00:20:12.968 --> 00:20:15.125
This is a real alarm.

00:20:15.125 --> 00:20:16.253
Please stay inside.

00:20:16.253 --> 00:20:18.990
- There's this funny thing
in international news

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:22.410
where in order for the world
to take notice of something,

00:20:22.410 --> 00:20:26.250
it sort of has to affect like Westerners.

00:20:26.250 --> 00:20:28.530
And that's no different with pirates.

00:20:28.530 --> 00:20:31.860
There was this one incident
in the fall of 2005

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:34.610
where a group of pirates showed
up to a luxury cruise ship.

00:20:34.610 --> 00:20:36.330
It was full of Western tourists.

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:41.183
- They hit us with rocket grenades, RPGs.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:48.180
There was a woman in her cabin,

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:51.450
and she was fortunately in her bathroom,

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:52.740
but a rocket grenade went right through

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:54.690
and blew the whole cabin up.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:56.070
- The cruise ship escaped,

00:20:56.070 --> 00:20:58.893
but it still led to global news coverage.

00:21:00.930 --> 00:21:02.700
Another incident was in 2008

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:05.823
when a Ukrainian ship was
hijacked by these pirates.

00:21:06.690 --> 00:21:08.190
Little did these pirates know

00:21:08.190 --> 00:21:10.110
they had just stumbled upon a shipment

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:13.830
of tanks and grenades and
ammunition bound for Sudan.

00:21:13.830 --> 00:21:17.100
They were demanding $35
million to give it back,

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.320
holding the crew and the ship hostage.

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:21.750
The U.S. and Russia both freaked out here,

00:21:21.750 --> 00:21:23.280
and they sent in their navys

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:24.720
to like monitor the situation

00:21:24.720 --> 00:21:25.553
to make sure the pirates

00:21:25.553 --> 00:21:27.240
weren't gonna take the
weapons off the boat.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:28.073
In the end,

00:21:28.073 --> 00:21:31.140
the pirates agreed to
a $3.2 million payout,

00:21:31.140 --> 00:21:33.510
and the secret weapons
shipment continued onward.

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:34.530
It was a big scare,

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:36.990
and it was another step
in waking the world up

00:21:36.990 --> 00:21:38.910
to how big of a deal this was becoming.

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:42.480
But the biggest event
happened a few months later.

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:45.900
A U.S. container ship was
traveling from Oman to Kenya

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.350
when four pirates boarded
and hijacked the ship,

00:21:49.350 --> 00:21:52.053
holding the crew hostage
and demanding $2 million.

00:21:55.011 --> 00:21:57.090
The U.S. government has a firm policy

00:21:57.090 --> 00:21:59.010
that it doesn't negotiate with hostages,

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:01.443
but what they do do is
send in their military.

00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:04.260
So after four days,

00:22:04.260 --> 00:22:06.540
the Navy Seals arrived with snipers.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:09.420
They shot three of the four
pirates and rescued the crew.

00:22:09.420 --> 00:22:11.490
It was an incident that was later depicted

00:22:11.490 --> 00:22:13.083
in this Tom Hanks movie.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:16.980
These were dramatic events,

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:20.580
a hostage situation with
snipers and Navy Seals,

00:22:20.580 --> 00:22:23.280
and it spread into the
international news cycle

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:26.700
in a way that Somali
piracy just hadn't before.

00:22:26.700 --> 00:22:28.200
- [Reporter] Captain
Phillips was taken hostage

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:29.457
for almost five days

00:22:29.457 --> 00:22:31.680
and then rescued by the U.S. Navy.

00:22:31.680 --> 00:22:33.390
- It was clear that this had become

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:35.220
too lucrative of an economy.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:37.710
There was too much incentive for warlords

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:40.410
to get in on all of this
money for it to stop.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:43.953
The only way to slow this down
was a serious intervention.

00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:47.430
As we were reporting this story,

00:22:47.430 --> 00:22:48.263
I kept asking like,

00:22:48.263 --> 00:22:50.670
"Why is this such a
difficult problem to solve?"

00:22:50.670 --> 00:22:52.770
And we asked all of the
experts we interviewed,

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:54.720
and we have a pretty good idea now

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:57.570
why this is way more
complicated than expected.

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:59.490
First off, guns.

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:01.200
You can't really carry guns

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:02.910
on international shipping vessels,

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:04.740
partly because there's
international gun laws

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:05.880
that are complicated,

00:23:05.880 --> 00:23:08.910
partly because shipping companies
don't want guns on boats

00:23:08.910 --> 00:23:11.100
because it can be a weird liability thing.

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:13.410
You can't just have like a
gun in a safe box somewhere.

00:23:13.410 --> 00:23:15.930
Second, it's hard to send in the navys

00:23:15.930 --> 00:23:17.250
to patrol these waters

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:20.580
because pirates often
don't look like pirates

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:21.870
until it's too late,

00:23:21.870 --> 00:23:23.370
at which point they're prone

00:23:23.370 --> 00:23:25.560
to use their victims as human shields

00:23:25.560 --> 00:23:27.060
against any threatening navy.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:29.550
So unless you've got like the
best snipers in the world,

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.290
like the Navy Seals,

00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:32.730
it's actually really hard to use

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.950
just like military force
against these pirates.

00:23:34.950 --> 00:23:38.100
And lastly, you have to remember
what we're talking about.

00:23:38.100 --> 00:23:39.930
We're talking about the open ocean,

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.180
huge swaths of water.

00:23:42.180 --> 00:23:43.170
It's really challenging

00:23:43.170 --> 00:23:45.840
to monitor and control
this in an effective way.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:47.670
It's one reason why piracy

00:23:47.670 --> 00:23:50.010
has always thrived on the open sea.

00:23:50.010 --> 00:23:51.930
But even despite all these challenges,

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:54.750
governments and businesses
put their heads together

00:23:54.750 --> 00:23:56.250
and they figured something out.

00:23:56.250 --> 00:23:58.320
The first one's kind of boring.

00:23:58.320 --> 00:23:59.790
It's this report

00:23:59.790 --> 00:24:02.820
that this international
shipping organization put out

00:24:02.820 --> 00:24:06.390
that basically teaches ships
and captains and companies

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:08.370
how to protect themselves.

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:11.250
They recommend them using
this high-pressure hose

00:24:11.250 --> 00:24:13.710
that allows 'em to just like
spray water at the pirates.

00:24:13.710 --> 00:24:14.700
They started recommending

00:24:14.700 --> 00:24:18.630
putting barbed wire and
razor wire around your ship.

00:24:18.630 --> 00:24:19.463
Kind of gnarly.

00:24:19.463 --> 00:24:21.600
Put locks on your doors,

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:23.070
increase your surveillance.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:24.540
Like this is pretty basic stuff.

00:24:24.540 --> 00:24:26.760
Create a safe room or a citadel

00:24:26.760 --> 00:24:30.184
where you can like lock
yourself in if you get hijacked.

00:24:30.184 --> 00:24:31.200
But one of the big things they did

00:24:31.200 --> 00:24:34.140
was create this
internationally-recognized corridor

00:24:34.140 --> 00:24:37.410
where ships could all travel
together like in a caravan,

00:24:37.410 --> 00:24:39.690
sometimes escorted by a navy ship

00:24:39.690 --> 00:24:40.770
from one of these countries,

00:24:40.770 --> 00:24:43.770
helping them navigate this really busy

00:24:43.770 --> 00:24:45.420
but very vulnerable part

00:24:45.420 --> 00:24:47.460
of the international shipping highway.

00:24:47.460 --> 00:24:49.230
And another huge step was,

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:51.780
in spite of all the complicated gun laws,

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:55.200
vessels were now allowed
to employ armed guards

00:24:55.200 --> 00:24:57.870
to stand watch as they
navigate these waters.

00:24:57.870 --> 00:24:59.730
So now you've got like security guards

00:24:59.730 --> 00:25:01.320
with guns on the ship

00:25:01.320 --> 00:25:02.610
who have nothing else to do

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:03.930
but like protect the boat.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:05.190
And guess what?

00:25:05.190 --> 00:25:08.400
All of these strategies totally worked.

00:25:08.400 --> 00:25:09.570
I mean, look at this graph.

00:25:09.570 --> 00:25:12.150
After its all-time high in 2011,

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:13.980
hijacking incidents plummeted

00:25:13.980 --> 00:25:15.573
because of these interventions.

00:25:19.170 --> 00:25:20.190
But this leaves us

00:25:20.190 --> 00:25:22.980
with kind of a complicated
resolution here.

00:25:22.980 --> 00:25:26.220
These communities were left
behind by their government.

00:25:26.220 --> 00:25:30.000
They don't have the
infrastructure of a society

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:32.250
where people can thrive and work.

00:25:32.250 --> 00:25:33.420
That's for a lot of reasons,

00:25:33.420 --> 00:25:35.850
but it's in part because of the pillaging

00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:38.100
and the exploitation from outsiders.

00:25:38.100 --> 00:25:39.540
And so while these warlords

00:25:39.540 --> 00:25:42.870
who committed horrific crimes to get rich

00:25:42.870 --> 00:25:45.540
deserve no moral justification,

00:25:45.540 --> 00:25:47.070
it is morally complicated

00:25:47.070 --> 00:25:49.140
when you look at people who
are just trying to live,

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:53.040
whose waters have been
poisoned by toxic waste,

00:25:53.040 --> 00:25:54.930
whose country has been pillaged,

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:59.850
and who turned to piracy as a last resort.

00:25:59.850 --> 00:26:02.160
But I'm not gonna go into
the ethical dilemma here.

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.290
I think you all can fight that out

00:26:04.290 --> 00:26:05.910
in the comments if you want.

00:26:05.910 --> 00:26:07.770
I'd love to see what you have to say.

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:11.370
The fact is piracy was
solved in the Horn of Africa

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:12.600
because of these interventions,

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:13.650
at least for the most part.

00:26:13.650 --> 00:26:16.440
But just as Somali piracy was declining,

00:26:16.440 --> 00:26:18.720
incidents of piracy started popping up

00:26:18.720 --> 00:26:20.250
on the other side of the continent.

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:22.560
The Gulf of Guinea took Somalia's title

00:26:22.560 --> 00:26:24.870
as the piracy hotspot of the world.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:27.150
These attacks happen
for different reasons,

00:26:27.150 --> 00:26:28.740
and they look quite different.

00:26:28.740 --> 00:26:30.720
They're usually Nigerian pirates

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:32.010
boarding oil tankers

00:26:32.010 --> 00:26:33.990
to steal the oil that they feel like

00:26:33.990 --> 00:26:35.430
was stolen from their land.

00:26:35.430 --> 00:26:38.250
The international community
is now turning its focus here,

00:26:38.250 --> 00:26:40.920
and this is a more
complicated thing to solve

00:26:40.920 --> 00:26:42.480
for a lot more reasons.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:45.030
The fact is piracy won't go away

00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:48.210
as long as the ingredients that
have always fueled it exist.

00:26:48.210 --> 00:26:51.450
Frustrated young men with
no chance of finding work

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:52.800
in an economy,

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:56.010
incentivized and organized by clan leaders

00:26:56.010 --> 00:26:58.230
looking to get rich on the high seas,

00:26:58.230 --> 00:26:59.730
operating in a gray area

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:01.350
where the government and the society

00:27:01.350 --> 00:27:04.110
that should support them and employ them

00:27:04.110 --> 00:27:06.150
doesn't exist or is too weak to help,

00:27:06.150 --> 00:27:08.490
leaving them to fend for themselves

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:11.910
and to turn to more and more
sophisticated forms of crime

00:27:11.910 --> 00:27:13.480
in the process.

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:17.230
(plaintive electronic music)

00:27:22.849 --> 00:27:26.599
